# Test OOM awareness

Always pay attention to out-of-memory (OOM) and memory-pressure risks when writing, editing, or running tests.

- Prefer focused test runs while iterating; use the full suite as a completion gate, not a tight loop.
- Avoid patterns that bloat heap across cases: unbounded fake-timer loops, leaking mounts, global stubs left active, giant fixtures duplicated per test.
- After `afterEach` / `afterAll`, restore timers, unstub globals/envs, and drop large mocks so cases do not accumulate memory.
- If a suite or CI job dies with OOM, SIGKILL, or unexplained hangs under load, treat memory as a first suspect—shrink scope, serialize heavy files, or split work before adding more coverage.
- Do not ignore rising RSS, worker crashes, or “JavaScript heap out of memory” when verifying changes.
